      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "$\\frac{8}{3}\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,Γ(\\frac{7}{2})+\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,Γ(\\frac{5}{2})=$",
      "worked_solution": "To evaluate the gamma function at multiples of one-half, we use the following recursive formula:\n\n$$\nΓ(z+1)=zΓ(z),\\,Γ(\\frac{1}{2})=\\sqrt{\\pi }\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following:\n\n$$\nΓ(\\frac{5}{2})=\\frac{3}{2}Γ(\\frac{3}{2})=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}Γ(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\pi }=\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{\\pi },\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ(\\frac{7}{2})=\\frac{5}{2}Γ(\\frac{5}{2})=\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{\\pi }=\\frac{15}{8}\\sqrt{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{3}\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,Γ(\\frac{7}{2})+\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,Γ(\\frac{5}{2})=\\frac{8}{3}\\sqrt{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{15}{8}\\sqrt{\\pi }+\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{\\pi }=6\\pi .\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
